<http://data.silknow.org/object/9ffed955-69d7-3c12-803c-cdde1df96dfd/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Disclaimer: while the term 'oriental' has been used in this record, it has since fallen from usage and is now considered offensive. The term is repeated in this record in its original historical context.\n\nFrom the 1840s, chatelaine bags described as 'somewhat Scotch and somewhat oriental' by the Englishwoman's Domestic Magazine of 1861 were increasingly popular, and hung from the waist belt by a cord or hook and chain.  By 1856 the fashion for vast skirts stretched over a crinoline rendered any pocket but the most diminutive unsightly.  Chatelaine bags were useful for coins and small items.  These were made of fabric or leather and usually fastened with a flap; many were made to match the dress.  They remained popular for the rest of the nineteenth century."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/9ffed955-69d7-3c12-803c-cdde1df96dfd> .

